// probe: n_array
#pragma plugin nvmidi

// The candidate, and the run that put it here.
//
// Run 36638827355 refused midi_input and midi_output as declared types while
// midi_note and midi_config declared without a word. Two of those four names
// were registered at a real size and two at 0 - but in both pairs one name
// worked and one did not, so size was never the common term and the size line
// was never the cause. The registrations differ in one other way, and it is
// the same way for both broken names and absent for both working ones:
//
//   midi_note    : RegisteredObjectType, properties, methods - no array
//   midi_config  : RegisteredObjectType, properties, methods - no array
//   midi_input   : ... then play_chord(array<midi_note@>@ notes)
//   midi_output  : ... then play_chord(array<midi_note@>@ notes)
//
// The array type is the engine's own registered script array, and a method
// declaration that names it can fail to register. This plugin registers
// nothing else that mentions it. The claim is therefore that the port type
// registration stops at the first array method, leaving the port name
// half-built in a way a script sees as "not a data type".
//
// This file does not test that claim and must not be read as testing it: the
// plugin it runs against has had the array methods left alone in the source
// and the size line changed, so a clean result here would not separate the
// two. It is a regression guard. If a future run shows these declarations
// accepted, the array is exonerated along with the size; if they are refused
// again, both candidates are alive and the next file to write is one that
// asks the engine for the array type alone, by name, with no port in it.
void main() {
	print("ARRAY_BEGIN");

	midi_message m;
	midi_duration u;
	midi_note@ n = null;
	midi_config@ c = null;
	midi_input@ in = null;
	midi_output@ out = null;
	print("ARRAY_DECLS_OK");

	print("ARRAY_END ");
}
